  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason includes the maximum and minimum outcomes of both lotteries. The individual explicitly considers the maximum outcome of Lottery B (0 Euros) and contrasts it with the fixed loss in Lottery A (-3000 Euros). Their justification hinges on the appeal of the more favorable maximum outcome (0 vs -3000), which aligns directly with the rule of the decision reason. Although they do not explicitly compare minimum outcomes, this is not required by the reason when maximum outcomes differ. The individual’s focus on the best possible result (0 Euros) as a decisive factor matches the logic of the decision reason. Therefore, both the information considered and the justification align closely with the decision reason’s structure and rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 100
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on the outcomes of the lotteries, specifically the maximum and minimum outcomes, and prefers the lottery with the more favorable (i.e., less negative or higher) maximum outcome. If the maximum outcomes are equal, it compares the minimum outcomes. In this case: Lottery A has outcomes of -3000 (25%) and 0 (75%), so its maximum outcome is 0 and minimum is -3000. Lottery B has outcomes of -4000 (20%) and 0 (80%), so its maximum outcome is 0 and minimum is -4000. Since both lotteries have the same maximum outcome (0), the reason would compare the minimum outcomes. The more favorable minimum outcome is -3000 (A), so the reason would prefer Lottery A.",
